    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "## Check 1: Equilibrium [N]\n\nThe reactions are the forces the supports must supply, $\\mathbf{R} = K\\mathbf{u} - \\mathbf{F}$ at the fixed degrees of freedom. Together they must cancel the 250 kN of deck load, and by symmetry each support takes half.",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "printf(true, \"Horizontal reaction at the pin: ~,3f kN~%\", R_pratt[1]/1000)$\nprintf(true, \"Vertical reactions: ~,3f kN and ~,3f kN~%\", R_pratt[2]/1000, R_pratt[3]/1000)$\nprintf(true, \"Sum of vertical reactions: ~,3f kN~%\", (R_pratt[2] + R_pratt[3])/1000)$",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 10,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "name": "stdout",
          "output_type": "stream",
          "text": [
            "Horizontal reaction at the pin: -0.000 kN\nVertical reactions: 125.000 kN and 125.000 kN\nSum of vertical reactions: 250.000 kN"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "## Check 2: The Method of Sections [S+N]\n\nCut the bridge through the third panel, between $x = 2d$ and $x = 3d$. The cut crosses the top chord $T_2$–$T_3$, the diagonal $T_2$–$B_3$ and the bottom chord $B_2$–$B_3$. Taking moments about $B_3$, where the diagonal and the bottom chord meet, eliminates both of them and leaves only the top chord, whose lever arm is the height $h$:\n\n$$F_{T_2T_3} = -\\frac{M(3d)}{h}$$\n\nwhere $M(3d)$ is the bending moment the loads and the left reaction produce at $B_3$.",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "R_left : 5*Pn/2$\nM_B3 : R_left*3*dp - Pn*(2*dp) - Pn*dp$\nF_top : ratsimp(-M_B3/hp);\nprintf(true, \"Method of sections: ~,3f kN~%\", float(subst([Pn = P_node, dp = d, hp = ht], F_top))/1000)$\nprintf(true, \"Stiffness method:   ~,3f kN~%\", N_pratt[sublist_indices(pratt, lambda([m], is(m = [T(2), T(3)])))[1]]/1000)$",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 11,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "name": "stdout",
          "output_type": "stream",
          "text": [
            "-((9*Pn*dp)/(2*hp))\nMethod of sections: -225.000 kN\nStiffness method:   -225.000 kN"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "The two methods agree. This bridge is statically determinate (21 members plus 3 reactions equal 24 equilibrium equations), so statics alone fixes the member forces, and the stiffness method must reproduce them whatever the cross-sections. The stiffness method also gives the displacements, and it works unchanged for indeterminate structures, where statics alone is not enough.\n\n## Pratt versus Howe [N]\n\nA Howe truss uses the same chords and verticals with the diagonals reversed. They now slope up towards the centre and carry compression, and the verticals carry tension instead. Long compression members can buckle, so steel bridges favour the Pratt layout and timber ones, which have stronger compression joints, historically used the Howe.",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "howe : append(chords, verticals, howe_diagonals)$\n[u_howe, R_howe] : solve_truss(howe)$\nN_howe : makelist(member_force(m, u_howe), m, howe)$\n\ndiag_forces(members, N) := makelist(N[k]/1000, k, length(members) - 3, length(members))$\nprintf(true, \"Pratt diagonals: ~{~8,1f~} kN~%\", diag_forces(pratt, N_pratt))$\nprintf(true, \"Howe diagonals:  ~{~8,1f~} kN~%\", diag_forces(howe, N_howe))$\nprintf(true, \"Midspan deflection: Pratt ~,2f mm, Howe ~,2f mm~%\", u_pratt[2*B(3)]*1000, u_howe[2*B(3)]*1000)$\n\ndraw_truss(howe, u_howe, N_howe,\n  \"Howe Truss: Deformed Shape (x50), Forces in kN\")$",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 12,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "name": "stdout",
          "output_type": "stream",
          "text": [
            "Pratt diagonals:    106.1    35.4    35.4   106.1 kN\nHowe diagonals:    -106.1   -35.4   -35.4  -106.1 kN\nMidspan deflection: Pratt -9.55 mm, Howe -10.45 mm"
          ]
        },
        {
          "data": {
            "application/x-maxima-plotly": [
